\subsection{NVE Normalization Robustness}
\label{sec:nve_robustness}

To assess sensitivity to baseline seed choice, we extend the single-seed baseline (seed~1, eval 915.7 at 105K) to three additional seeds trained to 505K steps (Table~\ref{tab:baseline_seeds}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Walker Walk baseline seeds at 105K and 505K steps.}
\label{tab:baseline_seeds}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Eval@105K} & \textbf{Eval@505K} & \textbf{ve@65K} & \textbf{ve@105K} \\
\midrule
s1 & 915.7 & 970.4 & 0.563 & 0.656 \\
s2 & 443.5 & 945.7 & 0.898 & 0.742 \\
s3 & 869.0 & 947.7 & --- & 0.664 \\
s4 & 922.8 & 938.9 & --- & 0.624 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Seed~2 exhibits slow convergence (eval 443.5 at 105K, recovering to 945.7 by 512K), confirming non-pathological slow convergence rather than seed failure. Its elevated $v/e$ ratio at 65K (0.898 vs.\ 0.563 for seed~1) reflects the low evaluation denominator rather than pathological value overestimation. Using seed~2 as the sole normalization reference systematically deflates NVE values, causing two marginal CIVO-positive configurations (008d seed~3, peak NVE $1.53\times$; 009b seed~2, peak NVE $1.62\times$) to fall below the $1.5\times$ threshold. Core CIVO cases (peak NVE $> 3\times$) remain correctly classified under all baseline seeds.

Single-seed normalization (s1) yields AUROC $= 0.933$ and accuracy 7/8 (87.5\%) on the Walker subset. Four-seed mean normalization yields AUROC $= 0.867$ and accuracy 6/8 (75.0\%): the boundary case exp\_009b flips from $\nve = 1.62\times$ (s1) to $1.23\times$ (4-seed mean), because its peak occurs at 45K when the s1 $v/e$ ratio (0.331) is well below the 4-seed mean (0.436). All high-NVE configurations remain correctly classified (006: $4.84\times \to 4.52\times$; 008d: $4.13\times \to 3.54\times$). The $1.5\times$ threshold thus correctly identifies all strong CIVO cases regardless of baseline choice; sensitivity is confined to configurations within 10\% of the threshold.

\paragraph{Normalization conventions.} Walker NVE is normalized against the 4-seed mean baseline (seeds~1--4, each run to 505K+ steps). Cheetah NVE is normalized against a 5-seed mean baseline (seeds~0--4, each run to ${\sim}$210K steps). The differing seed counts reflect available baseline data; both provide sufficient coverage of the respective experimental windows (Walker: 210K analysis window; Cheetah: 105K analysis window).

\paragraph{Limitations.} With $n = 10$ experiments, Clopper-Pearson confidence intervals remain wide (e.g., 90.0\% accuracy has 95\% CI [55.5\%, 99.7\%]). These results should be interpreted as a diagnostic proof-of-concept rather than a validated clinical threshold. Replication across additional environments and seeds is needed to establish tighter bounds. Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full} reports the original $n{=}16$ evaluation set spanning both environments. The expanded analysis ($n{=}29$, including SGS variants, RAVE, and TCN configurations) yields AUROC${=}0.953$ (Figure~\ref{fig:nve_roc}, in-sample, post-hoc). The original $n{=}16$ core analysis yields AUROC${=}0.969$; the expanded $n{=}29$ set includes SGS, RAVE, TCN, and dual-critic configurations. Cross-architecture validation is deferred to future work.

\subsection{Leave-One-Environment-Out Cross-Validation}
\label{sec:nve_loeo}

To assess whether $\theta{=}1.5\times$ generalizes beyond the in-sample evaluation, we perform leave-one-environment-out (LOEO) cross-validation across Walker ($n{=}13$), Cheetah ($n{=}5$), and PickCube ($n{=}2$). For each fold, we hold out all experiments from one environment, select the optimal accuracy-maximizing threshold range on the remaining training set, and evaluate the fixed $\theta{=}1.5\times$ on the held-out environment.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Leave-one-environment-out cross-validation of NVE threshold $\theta=1.5\times$.
For each fold, the threshold is validated on an environment whose data was \emph{not} used
to select $\theta$. The optimal threshold range on each training set is reported;
